    summary_en: "AMD has released its first true rack-scale AI computing platform, Helios, designed to compete with Nvidia's products. The system utilizes the MI455X GPU, based on AMD's fifth-generation CDNA architecture, and claims specifications that surpass Nvidia's Blackwell and Vera Rubin platforms across multiple metrics. Helios is designed using 2.5D and 3D packaging technologies; the MI455X chip integrates I/O, compute, and memory, featuring 24 chiplets. The compute core uses TSMC's 2nm process, stacked on 3nm Fabric and Cache chiplets. Compared to the previous generation MI355X, the MI455X offers up to a 4x performance increase for AI workloads, focusing on low-precision data types such as MXFP4 and MXFP8. At the system level, Helios uses Ultra Accelerator Link over Ethernet (UALoE) for expansion and is equipped with the Pensando Vulcano network card, providing 2.4 Tbps of expansion bandwidth per accelerator, which surpasses Nvidia's ConnectX-9. AMD anticipates that Helios will deliver up to a 30% performance improvement per dollar, challenging the status quo of the data center AI computing market.",
