    summary_en: "This article reports that cybersecurity experts point out that persistent attack activities originating from Russia have continued for years, utilizing email as the primary infection vector. The attack methods highly mimic normal communications, aiming to bypass traditional mail filtering mechanisms by embedding malicious payloads into seemingly harmless email content. The practical impact of these attacks is enormous; they can not only steal users' sensitive information but also spread malware within corporate internal networks. Given the ubiquity and difficulty of detection of the attack vector, organizations are advised to strengthen email security training and consider implementing multi-layered email content analysis and behavioral anomaly detection systems, rather than relying solely on traditional attachment scanning.",
